---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SuiteToggle from '../components/SuiteToggle.astro';
import DivisionToggle from '../components/DivisionToggle.astro';
import ExportButtons from '../components/ExportButtons.astro';
---
<Base title="Peira leaderboard, adversarial robustness of decision models" current="Leaderboard">
  <div data-page="leaderboard">
    <div class="wrap">
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="kicker">Adversarial robustness benchmark</div>
        <h1>Which decision models <span class="amber">hold up</span> under attack</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Peira measures how often an attacker can flip a decision model from its correct
          answer. Lower attack success rate means a more robust model. Every number below
          traces to a sealed run artifact, and every confidence interval is a 95% interval.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="controls">
        <SuiteToggle />
        <DivisionToggle />
        <label><input type="checkbox" id="ineligible-filter" /> Hide runs that are not ranking eligible</label>
        <span class="count" id="board-count"></span>
      </div>

      <div class="table-scroll" id="board-wrap" aria-live="polite"></div>

      <ExportButtons />

      <section class="prose">
        <h2>How to read this table</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Conditional ASR</strong> is the headline metric. It is the share of eligible
          cases where the attacked run produced a different decision than the benign run.
          Conditional means the denominator only includes cases where the model got the
          benign case right, so a model cannot look robust by failing the easy cases.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Benign accuracy</strong> is the share of benign cases decided correctly.
          A model with low ASR but poor benign accuracy is not useful, so read the two together.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Ranking eligible</strong> marks runs that passed every validity gate.
          Runs that failed a gate stay visible for transparency with a not eligible mark.
          Tick the checkbox above to hide them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Divisions.</strong> The board has two divisions, guardrails and
          structured-output LLM baselines, shown side by side. Every submission
          declares its division and the declaration is sealed into the run artifact,
          so the board can check it. Ranks are dense within each division. A guardrail
          and a raw LLM baseline are not the same kind of thing, and the headline
          ranking does not pretend they are.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          The Public suite runs on the public case set. The Holdout suite runs on a private
          case set the adapters never see, which guards against overfitting to the public cases.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
